      Title Optimization model for Glen Canyon Dam releases to favor Bugs popula
      tion. (June 2018)
       
      ###################################
      Created By: Moazzam Ali Rind
      Email: moazzamalirind@gmail.com
       
      Created : 6/15/2020
      Last updated: 10/13/2020
       
      Description: This model was developed to qaunitfy the trade-off between nu
      mber of steady low flow days and hydropower revenue objectives.
                  The model has 2 periods per day (i.e. pHigh and plow) and runs
       for a month. we have used linear programming to solve the problem.
                  All the structural and operational constraints applied here ar
      e uptodate.
       
      ######################################
       
  19   
  20  ****Model code:
  21   
  22  Set
  23   
  24            d                             days in June
  25            p                             time period during a day /pLow "Lo
      w flow period",pHigh "High flow period"/
  26            modpar                        Saving model parameter for each of
       the scenario/ ModStat "Model Status", SolStat "Solve Status"/
  27  ;
  28   
  29   
  30  *======================================
  31  *Parameters
  32  *======================================
  33   
  34  PARAMETERS
  35   
  36  FStore                           Storing objective function values
  37   
  38  XStore_steady                    Store Energy Generated during steady flow
       days (MWH)
  39  XStore_unsteady                  Store Energy Generated during unsteady fl
      ow days  (MWH)
  40   
  41  RStore_steady(p)                 Store Release values during steady flow d
      ays for each period (cfs)
  42  RStore_unsteady(p)               Store Release values during unsteady flow
       days for each period(cfs)
  43   
  44  Sstore                           Store Storage Values (ac-ft)
  45   
  46  ModelResults(modpar)             Store solution status of the scenarios i.
      e. whether the solution found is optimal or not?
  47  release(p)                       Average release observed during each peri
      od (p) over the month (cfs) /pLow 10840.07, pHigh 15738.38/
  48  * The pLow release value is the average of releases observed over the mont
      h during low periods.Also, weekneds are considered low periods. Whereas, t
      he release value for pHigh is average of releases during the high periods 
      over the month's time.
  49  Steady_Release                   Steady release value of the hydrograph /1
      0840.07/
  50  **Steady release is for weekends. plow across week= steady release (model 
      assumption).
  51   
  52  initstorage                           Initial reservoir storage on  June 1
      ST 2018 (acre-ft)
  53  maxstorage                            Reservoir capacity (acre-ft)
  54  minstorage                            Minimum reservoir storage to maintai
      n hydropower level(acre-ft)
  55  Inflow(d)                             Inflow to reservoir (cfs)
  56  maxRel                                Maximum release in a day d at any ti
      meperiod p(cfs)
  57  minRel                                Minimum release in a day d at any ti
      meperiod p(cfs)
  58  evap                                  evaporation (ac-ft per day Considere
      d constant throughout the month
  59  EnergyRate(p)                         Energy prices on weekdays ($ per MWH
      ) /pLow 37.7002, pHigh 63.5172/
  60  weekendRate(p)                        Energy prices on weekends ($ per MWH
      ) /pLow 37.7002, pHigh 37.7002/
  61  Duration(p)                           Duration of period (hours)
  62  Steady_Days                           The number of steady low flow days (
      observed during June 2018)
  63  ;
  64   
  65   
  66   
  67  Duration("pLow")= 8;
  68  * low period weightage in a day(08 Hours or 8 by 24 i.e:0.33 of day)
  69   
  70  Duration("pHigh")= 16;
  71  *  High period weightage in a day( 16 Hours or 16 by 24 i.e:0.67 of day)
  72   
  73  *===================================================
  74  * Read data from Excel
  75  *===================================================
  77   
  78  *Write the input Data into a GDX file
GDXIN   E:\OneDrive - Washington State University (email.wsu.edu)\Documents\GitH
        ub\BugFlowExperiment_Analysis2026\Validation\June\Weekend-Weekday&Hourly
        \Valid_June2018(2Periods).gdx
  80   
  81  * parameters and input data from the GDX file into the model
--- LOAD  d = 1:d
--- LOAD  Inflow = 2:Inflow
--- LOAD  initstorage = 3:initstorage
--- LOAD  maxstorage = 4:maxstorage
--- LOAD  minstorage = 5:minstorage
--- LOAD  maxRel = 6:maxRel
--- LOAD  minRel = 7:minRel
--- LOAD  evap = 8:evap
  90   
  91  *Close the GDX file
  93   
  94  Display d,inflow, initstorage, maxstorage, minstorage, maxRel, minRel, eva
      p, p, Duration;
  95  *===============================================
  96  SCALAR
  97  Convert                       conversion factor from cfs to ac-ft per hour
       (0.0014*60)/0.083/
  98  Totaldays                      Total number of days in the month/30/
  99  weekdays                      Number of days in month/21/
 100  weekends                       Number of weekend days in the month /9/
 101  Num_of_timesteps              Total Number of timesteps used /60/
 102  Daily_Ramprate                Allowable daily ramp rate (cfs)/8000/
 103   
 104   
 105  VARIABLES
 106   
 107  ObjectiveVal                   Objective function value
 108  storage                        Reservoir storage at the end of the month (
      acre-ft)
 109  Energy_Gen(p)                  Hydropower Generated within a period during
       unsteady flow day (MWH)
 110  SteadyEn_Gen(p)                Hydropower Generated within a period during
       steady flow days (MWH)
 111  Avail_Water                    Total Water available in the system during 
      the month (June)
 112  steady_Outflow                 Volume of water released during steady low 
      flow days (acre-ft)
 113  unsteady_Outflow               Volume of water released during unsteay flo
      w days (acre-ft)
 114  TotMonth_volume                Total monthly volume released (acre-ft)
 115   
 116  ;
 117   
 118   
 119   
 120  EQUATIONS
 121  *AND CONSTRAINTS
 122   
 123  EQa_Inflow                   Total volume of Water available in the system
       during the month (acre-ft)
 124  EQb_SteadyOutflow            Volume of Water released during the steady da
      ys (acre-ft)
 125  EQc_UnSteadyOutflow          Volume of Water released during the unsteady 
      days (acre-ft)
 126   
 127  EQ1__ResMassBal              Reservoir mass balance (acre-ft)
 128  EQ2__reqpowerstorage         The minimum storage equivalent to reservoir l
      evel required for hydropower generation (acre-ft)
 129  EQ3__maxstor                 res storage max (acre-ft)
 130  EQ4__MaxR(p)                 Max Release (cfs)
 131  EQ5__MinR(p)                 Min Release  (cfs)
 132  EQ6_Rampup_rate(p)           Constraining the daily ramp up rate between t
      he timesteps within same day (cfs)
 133  EQ7__Monthtlyrel             Total monthly volume released (acre-ft)
 134  EQ8_SteadyEnergy(p)          Energy generated within each period during st
      eady flow day (MWH)
 135  EQ9_EnergyGen(p)             Energy generated within each period during un
      steady flow day (MWH)
 136  EQ10_EnergyGen_Max(p)        Maximum Energy Generation Limit of the Glen C
      aynon Dam(MW)
 137  EQ11_EnergyRevenue           Total monthly Hydropower Revenue generated wh
      en number of steady low flow day are greater than number of weekend days($
      )
 138  EQ12_Revenue                 Total monthly Hydropower Renvenue generated i
      f number of steady flow days are equal or less than weekend days ($)
 139  ;
 140   
 141  *-------------------------------------------------------------------------
      -----*
 142   
 143   
 144  EQa_Inflow..                 Avail_Water =e= initstorage + sum(d,inflow(d)
      *Convert*sum(p,Duration(p)));
 145  EQb_SteadyOutflow..          steady_Outflow =e= Steady_Release*sum(p,Durat
      ion(p))*Convert*Steady_Days;
 146  EQc_UnSteadyOutflow..        unsteady_Outflow =e= sum(p,release(p)*Convert
      *Duration(p))*(Totaldays-Steady_Days);
 147   
 148   
 149  EQ1__ResMassBal..           storage =e= Avail_Water-steady_Outflow-unstead
      y_Outflow-(evap*Totaldays);
 150  EQ2__reqpowerstorage..      storage =g= minstorage;
 151  EQ3__maxstor..              storage =l= maxstorage;
 152   
 153  EQ4__MaxR(p)..              release(p)=l= maxRel;
 154  EQ5__MinR(p)..              release(p)=g= minRel;
 155  EQ6_Rampup_rate(p)..         release("pHigh")-release("pLow")=l=Daily_Ramp
      rate;
 156   
 157  *EQ7_  constraining the overall monthly released volume..
 158  EQ7__Monthtlyrel..                         TotMonth_volume=e= steady_Outfl
      ow + unsteady_Outflow;
 159   
 160   
 161  EQ8_SteadyEnergy(p)..                      SteadyEn_Gen(p)=e= Steady_Relea
      se*Duration(p)*0.03715;
 162  * Energy generation formula used in wapa Execl model.
 163   
 164  EQ9_EnergyGen(p)..                         Energy_Gen(p)=e= release(p)*Dur
      ation(p)*0.03715;
 165  * Energy generation formula used in wapa Execl model..
 166   
 167  EQ10_EnergyGen_Max(p)..                    Energy_Gen(p)=l= 1320*Duration(
      p);
 168  *Maximum Energy Generation capacity of GCD (MWH).. Source https://www.usbr
      .gov/uc/rm/crsp/gc/
 169   
 170  **EQ12_HyrdroPower objective                                              
                                                           Energy generated from
       the period of low steady flow multiply by the energy rate and summed over
       the day (2 periods) in a day and multiply by the number of steady low flo
      w days.
 171  EQ11_EnergyRevenue..                         ObjectiveVal=e= sum(p,SteadyE
      n_Gen(p)*weekendRate(p))*weekends + sum(p,SteadyEn_Gen(p)*EnergyRate(p))*(
      Steady_Days-weekends)+ sum(p,Energy_Gen(p)*EnergyRate(p))*(Totaldays-Stead
      y_Days);
 172  * *This equation works for number of steady days greater than number of we
      ekend days.
 173   
 174  EQ12_Revenue..                             ObjectiveVal=e= sum(p,SteadyEn_
      Gen(p)*weekendRate(p))*(Steady_Days) + sum(p,Energy_Gen(p)*weekendRate(p))
      *(weekends - Steady_Days)+ sum(p,Energy_Gen(p)*EnergyRate(p))*(Totaldays-w
      eekends);
 175  *This equation works for number of steady days either equal or less than w
      eekend days.  This equation price all the weekend days by weekned rate irr
      espective of whether its steady or hydropeaking day.
 176   
 177  *-------------------------------------------------------------------------
      -----*
 178  ***************************************************
 179  ******Linear Model Defination
 180  ****************************************** ********
 181   
 182  Steady_Days = 9;
 183   
 184  MODEL Model1 Find value of hydropower revenue using LP/EQa_Inflow,EQb_Stea
      dyOutflow,EQc_UnSteadyOutflow,EQ1__ResMassBal,EQ2__reqpowerstorage,EQ3__ma
      xstor,EQ4__MaxR,EQ5__MinR,EQ6_Rampup_rate,EQ7__Monthtlyrel,EQ8_SteadyEnerg
      y,EQ9_EnergyGen,EQ10_EnergyGen_Max,EQ11_EnergyRevenue/;
 185  *this Model will work under scenarios when number of steady low bug flow d
      ays are greater than number of weekned days.
 186   
 187  Model Model2 Find value of hydropower revenue using LP /EQa_Inflow,EQb_Ste
      adyOutflow,EQc_UnSteadyOutflow,EQ1__ResMassBal,EQ2__reqpowerstorage,EQ3__m
      axstor,EQ4__MaxR,EQ5__MinR,EQ6_Rampup_rate,EQ7__Monthtlyrel,EQ8_SteadyEner
      gy,EQ9_EnergyGen,EQ10_EnergyGen_Max,EQ12_Revenue/;
 188  *this Model will work under scenarios when number of steady low bug flow d
      ays are less than or equal to number of weekned days.
 189   
 190   
 191  *Defining which solver to use. One can change it to any solver having capa
      bility to solve linear problem
 192  option LP= BARON;
 193   
 194   
 195  * solve statement
 196  if  (Steady_Days > weekends,
 197       SOLVE Model1 USING LP maximize ObjectiveVal;
 198  else SOLVE Model2 USING LP maximize ObjectiveVal;
 199  );
 200   
 201   
 202  *Following lines of code are storing solution values for different paramet
      ers
 203  FStore= ObjectiveVal.L;
 204  XStore_steady= (Steady_Release*sum(p,Duration(p))*0.03715)*Steady_Days;
 205  XStore_unsteady= sum(p,release(p)*Duration(p)*0.03715)*(Totaldays-Steady_D
      ays);
 206  RStore_steady(p)= Steady_Release;
 207  RStore_unsteady(p)= release(p);
 208  Sstore=storage.L;
 209  ModelResults("SolStat")= Model2.solvestat;
 210  ModelResults("ModStat")= Model2.modelstat;
 211   
 212  DISPLAY FStore,XStore_steady,XStore_unsteady,RStore_steady,RStore_unsteady
      ,Sstore;
 213   
 214   
 215  *-------------------------------------------------------------------------
      -----*
 216  * Dump all input data and results to a GAMS gdx file
 217  Execute_Unload "Valid_June2018(2Periods).gdx";
 218  * Dump the gdx file to an Excel workbook
 219  Execute "gdx2xls Valid_June2018(2Periods).gdx"
 220   
